data: {"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Validated recipe — viem@2.55.19\n\n⚠ Version disagreement: this recipe was validated against `viem@2.55.19`, while the units below are labelled `viem@2`. Both are stated rather than reconciled — a recipe's stamp records what was actually run, and a unit's label records what the corpus holds. Differences inside one major are not always cosmetic: check the recipe's own pins before copying it against these units.\n_human-validated 2026-08-19 by farseen (github: FarseenSh)_\n_source: recipes/viem/contract-call.yaml_\n\n**Receipts** — what was actually run, on 2026-08-19, by farseen (github: FarseenSh)\n- checks: attest, test, fork\n- chains exercised: none\n- on-chain:\n  - none recorded — this row exercises no chain, or its run broadcast nothing. A stated absence, never a gap: the receipt for such a row IS the suite result.\n\n# Recipe: viem @2 :: contract-call\n# STATUS: ✅ VALIDATED 2026-08-19 by farseen (github: FarseenSh) — 7 quotes · 10 structural · 5 chain assertions on a local EVM, run by the curator on his own machine. ⭐ All gas figures came back BYTE-IDENTICAL to the author run on a different machine — these suites read no chain state, so that agreement is measured, not assumed.\nproject_id: \"viem\"                     # the corpus id, as artifacts/p1-projects-v1.json has it\nversion_major: 2                       # §2's ordinary meaning: a real semver major of one package\nversion: \"2.55.19\"                     # ⚠ THE EXACT VERSION INSTALLED AND EXECUTED, in this\n                                       #   recipe's OWN isolated install. The repository root\n                                       #   pins viem 2.55.11 for twenty already-stamped recipes\n                                       #   and is deliberately NOT bumped — see mistake #5 and\n                                       #   test/viem/package.json's own description field.\ntype: \"contract-call\"\n\n# ⚠ NO CHAIN IS EXERCISED, AND THE EMPTY LIST IS THE HONEST VALUE (SCHEMA-DEPARTURES §4).\n# The chain-side checks run against a LOCAL anvil (chain id 31337) holding a token this suite\n# compiles and deploys itself. No RPC, no key, no funds, no broadcast. The tempting fill is\n# `[ethereum]` because the code obviously works there — that would claim an exercise nobody\n# performed. Precedent: recipes/openzeppelin-contracts, the first row in this repo to do this.\nchains: []\n\n# ── DEPLOYMENTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ndeployments: {}\n\n# ── WORKING EXAMPLE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nworking_example:\n  source_protocol: \"viem\"\n  source_url: \"https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01/src/actions/getContract.ts\"\n  code: |\n    // ── contract-call.ts ──\n    // Call a contract with viem 2.55.19: read, simulate, write, wait.\n    //\n    // ⚠ The `publicClient` / `walletClient` parameters belong to viem 1.21.4. They were REMOVED\n    //   in 2.0.0 and do not exist at the 2.55.19 this is written against; `client` replaced both.\n    import {\n      createPublicClient,\n      createWalletClient,\n      getAddress,\n      getContract,\n      http,\n      isAddress,\n      parseAbi,\n      type Address,\n      type Chain,\n      type Hex,\n    } from 'viem'\n    import { privateKeyToAccount } from 'viem/accounts'\n\n    /** The three ERC-20 members this recipe touches. Nothing wider is needed to make a call. */\n    export const ERC20_ABI = parseAbi([\n      'function balanceOf(address) view returns (uint256)',\n      'function decimals() view returns (uint8)',\n      'function transfer(address to, uint256 value) returns (bool)',\n    ])\n\n    export function clients(chain: Chain, rpcUrl: string, privateKey: Hex) {\n      const publicClient = createPublicClient({ chain, transport: http(rpcUrl) })\n      const walletClient = createWalletClient({\n        account: privateKeyToAccount(privateKey),\n        chain,\n        transport: http(rpcUrl),\n      })\n      return { publicClient, walletClient }\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Normalise an address a user or an API handed you.\n     *\n     * ⚠ DO NOT REACH FOR `isAddress` HERE. At viem 2.55.19 `isAddress` compares against the\n     *   EIP-55 checksum by default, so an all-uppercase or wrongly-cased spelling of a perfectly\n     *   real address returns FALSE — while `getAddress` accepts all of them and returns the same\n     *   canonical address. viem's validator and viem's normaliser disagree, and the normaliser\n     *   is the one that matches what the chain thinks. `isAddress` at viem 1.21.4 took no options\n     *   and accepted any 40 hex digits, which is why code written against viem 1.21.4 reads as if this is safe.\n     */\n    export function normalise(input: string): Address {\n      return getAddress(input) // throws InvalidAddressError on anything that is not an address\n    }\n\n    /** `isAddress` used the ONE way it is not a trap: as a boolean guard, casing-insensitive. */\n    export function looksLikeAnAddress(input: string): boolean {\n      return isAddress(input, { strict: false })\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * A contract instance.\n     *\n     * ⭐ `client` is the viem 2 parameter. Pass ONE client when it can do the job, or\n     *    `{ public, wallet }` when reads and writes come from two different clients.\n     */\n    export function erc20(\n      address: Address,\n      clientPair: { public: ReturnType<typeof clients>['publicClient']; wallet: ReturnType<typeof clients>['walletClient'] },\n    ) {\n      return getContract({ address, abi: ERC20_ABI, client: clientPair })\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Send a transfer the way viem intends: SIMULATE, then write the simulation's own request.\n     *\n     * ⚠ `simulateContract` returns `{ request, result }` and `request` is what `writeContract`\n     *   takes. Rebuilding the arguments by hand instead is how a call that simulated cleanly\n     *   goes on to send something slightly different.\n     */\n    export async function transfer(\n      token: Address,\n      to: Address,\n      value: bigint,\n      { publicClient, walletClient }: ReturnType<typeof clients>,\n    ) {\n      const { request, result } = await publicClient.simulateContract({\n        account: walletClient.account,\n        address: token,\n        abi: ERC20_ABI,\n        functionName: 'transfer',\n        args: [to, value],\n      })\n      const hash = await walletClient.writeContract(request)\n      // ⚠ A HASH IS NOT A RESULT. The transaction is not mined, and may revert once it is.\n      const receipt = await publicClient.waitForTransactionReceipt({ hash })\n      return { hash, receipt, simulated: result }\n    }\n\n# ── INTERFACE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ninterface:\n  lang: \"typescript\"\n  body: |\n    // viem 2.55.19 — the surface a contract call actually touches.\n    // Everything marked viem 1.21.4 below is REAL, and real at a version this is not.\n\n    // ── clients ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n_… recipe truncated for budget — 32035 bytes total, full text at the source path above._\n_Continue it: `web3_fetch` with `cursor: \"recipe:viem@2.55.19::contract-call::body@6140\"` — each call returns the next slice and the next cursor until there is none._\n\n**Citations** (preserved through truncation — degrade bodies, never citations):\n- https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01/src/actions/getContract.ts\n- https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01/site/pages/docs/migration-guide.mdx\n- https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/viem%401.21.4/src/actions/getContract.ts\n- https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01/src/CHANGELOG.md\n- https://github.com/wevm/viem/tree/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01\n\n---\n\n### 5e9009de-43b4-5998-82ae-95f6d76a66c3\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/getting-started.mdx#L32-L32\n\n```\nGetting Started [Get started with viem in just a few lines of code.] › Quick Start\n```\n\n### dd0a7780-ce34-5d9d-8cf7-85763fed3a0d\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/getting-started.mdx#L1-L1\n\n```\nGetting Started [Get started with viem in just a few lines of code.]\n```\n\n### 5318bd5a-4c42-5726-98cb-57b3aeb4c484\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/getting-started.mdx#L14-L30\n\n```\nGetting Started [Get started with viem in just a few lines of code.] › Installation\n:::code-group\n\n```bash [npm]\nnpm i viem\n```\n\n```bash [pnpm]\npnpm i viem\n```\n\n```bash [bun]\nbun i viem\n```\n\n:::\n```\n\n### 39ac0bb4-dc09-5390-ad89-fdc5f53ce65b\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/getting-started.mdx#L62-L68\n\n```\nGetting Started [Get started with viem in just a few lines of code.] › Quick Start › 2. Consume Actions\nconst client = createPublicClient({\n  chain: mainnet,\n  transport: http(),\n})\n\nconst blockNumber = await client.getBlockNumber() // [!code focus]\n```\n```\n\n### 53189a49-51d4-59e4-9c00-338a0b800b98\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/getting-started.mdx#L53-L55\n\n```\nGetting Started [Get started with viem in just a few lines of code.] › Quick Start › 2. Consume Actions\nNow that you have a Client set up, you can now interact with Ethereum and consume [Actions](/docs/actions/public/introduction)!\n```\n\n### 2ddb4309-255e-583d-b192-cfba74b27801\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/getting-started.mdx#L34-L36\n\n```\nGetting Started [Get started with viem in just a few lines of code.] › Quick Start › 1. Set up your Client & Transport\nFirstly, set up your [Client](/docs/clients/intro) with a desired [Transport](/docs/clients/intro) & [Chain](/docs/chains/introduction).\n```\n\n### 1e009c6c-eaf9-5b11-8eda-1ce06c9f2033\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/glossary/errors.md#L62-L62\n\n```\nErrors [Glossary of Errors in viem.] › Contract › `RawContractError`\n```\n\n### 40787796-d60e-57e5-b616-a809efefe778\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/getting-started.mdx#L70-L72\n\n```\nGetting Started [Get started with viem in just a few lines of code.] › Live example\n<iframe className=\"mt-6\" width=\"100%\" height=\"600px\" frameBorder=\"0\" src=\"https://stackblitz.com/edit/viem-getting-started?embed=1&file=index.ts&hideNavigation=1&hideDevTools=true&terminalHeight=1&hideExplorer=1&dev…\n```\n\n### f8ee5a27-2486-5e5b-84c7-3c5e9d9f2b1b\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/glossary/errors.md#L59-L59\n\n```\nErrors [Glossary of Errors in viem.] › Contract › `ContractFunctionExecutionError`\n```\n\n### 322e698e-cae9-598e-b194-2aa30c032990\n`viem@2` · ⚠ version_defaulted · repo:88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f · as_of 2026-08-02T23:42:13Z\nhttps://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/88d68aa3896c88930c84387ca4145653a7c9615f/site/pages/docs/glossary/errors.md#L60-L60\n\n```\nErrors [Glossary of Errors in viem.] › Contract › `ContractFunctionRevertedError`\n```"}],"structuredContent":{"intent":"integrate","recipe":{"project_id":"viem","version":"2.55.19","recipe_type":"contract-call","last_validated":"2026-08-19","validated_by":"farseen (github: FarseenSh)","receipts":{"validated_on":"2026-08-19","validated_by":"farseen (github: FarseenSh)","checks":["attest","test","fork"],"chains_exercised":[],"on_chain":"none recorded — this row exercises no chain, or its run broadcast nothing. A stated absence, never a gap: the receipt for such a row IS the suite result."},"body":"# Recipe: viem @2 :: contract-call\n# STATUS: ✅ VALIDATED 2026-08-19 by farseen (github: FarseenSh) — 7 quotes · 10 structural · 5 chain assertions on a local EVM, run by the curator on his own machine. ⭐ All gas figures came back BYTE-IDENTICAL to the author run on a different machine — these suites read no chain state, so that agreement is measured, not assumed.\nproject_id: \"viem\"                     # the corpus id, as artifacts/p1-projects-v1.json has it\nversion_major: 2                       # §2's ordinary meaning: a real semver major of one package\nversion: \"2.55.19\"                     # ⚠ THE EXACT VERSION INSTALLED AND EXECUTED, in this\n                                       #   recipe's OWN isolated install. The repository root\n                                       #   pins viem 2.55.11 for twenty already-stamped recipes\n                                       #   and is deliberately NOT bumped — see mistake #5 and\n                                       #   test/viem/package.json's own description field.\ntype: \"contract-call\"\n\n# ⚠ NO CHAIN IS EXERCISED, AND THE EMPTY LIST IS THE HONEST VALUE (SCHEMA-DEPARTURES §4).\n# The chain-side checks run against a LOCAL anvil (chain id 31337) holding a token this suite\n# compiles and deploys itself. No RPC, no key, no funds, no broadcast. The tempting fill is\n# `[ethereum]` because the code obviously works there — that would claim an exercise nobody\n# performed. Precedent: recipes/openzeppelin-contracts, the first row in this repo to do this.\nchains: []\n\n# ── DEPLOYMENTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ndeployments: {}\n\n# ── WORKING EXAMPLE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nworking_example:\n  source_protocol: \"viem\"\n  source_url: \"https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01/src/actions/getContract.ts\"\n  code: |\n    // ── contract-call.ts ──\n    // Call a contract with viem 2.55.19: read, simulate, write, wait.\n    //\n    // ⚠ The `publicClient` / `walletClient` parameters belong to viem 1.21.4. They were REMOVED\n    //   in 2.0.0 and do not exist at the 2.55.19 this is written against; `client` replaced both.\n    import {\n      createPublicClient,\n      createWalletClient,\n      getAddress,\n      getContract,\n      http,\n      isAddress,\n      parseAbi,\n      type Address,\n      type Chain,\n      type Hex,\n    } from 'viem'\n    import { privateKeyToAccount } from 'viem/accounts'\n\n    /** The three ERC-20 members this recipe touches. Nothing wider is needed to make a call. */\n    export const ERC20_ABI = parseAbi([\n      'function balanceOf(address) view returns (uint256)',\n      'function decimals() view returns (uint8)',\n      'function transfer(address to, uint256 value) returns (bool)',\n    ])\n\n    export function clients(chain: Chain, rpcUrl: string, privateKey: Hex) {\n      const publicClient = createPublicClient({ chain, transport: http(rpcUrl) })\n      const walletClient = createWalletClient({\n        account: privateKeyToAccount(privateKey),\n        chain,\n        transport: http(rpcUrl),\n      })\n      return { publicClient, walletClient }\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Normalise an address a user or an API handed you.\n     *\n     * ⚠ DO NOT REACH FOR `isAddress` HERE. At viem 2.55.19 `isAddress` compares against the\n     *   EIP-55 checksum by default, so an all-uppercase or wrongly-cased spelling of a perfectly\n     *   real address returns FALSE — while `getAddress` accepts all of them and returns the same\n     *   canonical address. viem's validator and viem's normaliser disagree, and the normaliser\n     *   is the one that matches what the chain thinks. `isAddress` at viem 1.21.4 took no options\n     *   and accepted any 40 hex digits, which is why code written against viem 1.21.4 reads as if this is safe.\n     */\n    export function normalise(input: string): Address {\n      return getAddress(input) // throws InvalidAddressError on anything that is not an address\n    }\n\n    /** `isAddress` used the ONE way it is not a trap: as a boolean guard, casing-insensitive. */\n    export function looksLikeAnAddress(input: string): boolean {\n      return isAddress(input, { strict: false })\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * A contract instance.\n     *\n     * ⭐ `client` is the viem 2 parameter. Pass ONE client when it can do the job, or\n     *    `{ public, wallet }` when reads and writes come from two different clients.\n     */\n    export function erc20(\n      address: Address,\n      clientPair: { public: ReturnType<typeof clients>['publicClient']; wallet: ReturnType<typeof clients>['walletClient'] },\n    ) {\n      return getContract({ address, abi: ERC20_ABI, client: clientPair })\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Send a transfer the way viem intends: SIMULATE, then write the simulation's own request.\n     *\n     * ⚠ `simulateContract` returns `{ request, result }` and `request` is what `writeContract`\n     *   takes. Rebuilding the arguments by hand instead is how a call that simulated cleanly\n     *   goes on to send something slightly different.\n     */\n    export async function transfer(\n      token: Address,\n      to: Address,\n      value: bigint,\n      { publicClient, walletClient }: ReturnType<typeof clients>,\n    ) {\n      const { request, result } = await publicClient.simulateContract({\n        account: walletClient.account,\n        address: token,\n        abi: ERC20_ABI,\n        functionName: 'transfer',\n        args: [to, value],\n      })\n      const hash = await walletClient.writeContract(request)\n      // ⚠ A HASH IS NOT A RESULT. The transaction is not mined, and may revert once it is.\n      const receipt = await publicClient.waitForTransactionReceipt({ hash })\n      return { hash, receipt, simulated: result }\n    }\n\n# ── INTERFACE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ninterface:\n  lang: \"typescript\"\n  body: |\n    // viem 2.55.19 — the surface a contract call actually touches.\n    // Everything marked viem 1.21.4 below is REAL, and real at a version this is not.\n\n    // ── clients ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────","source_path":"recipes/viem/contract-call.yaml","requested_version":null,"exact_version_match":null,"body_truncated":true,"body_bytes":32035,"citations":["https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01/src/actions/getContract.ts","https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01/site/pages/docs/migration-guide.mdx","https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/viem%401.21.4/src/actions/getContract.ts","https://github.com/wevm/viem/blob/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01/src/CHANGELOG.md","https://github.com/wevm/viem/tree/cfc1ae59600df2cdc8dc7d56ac645362aac22d01"],"citations_capped":false,"body_cursor":"recipe:viem@2.55.19::contract-call::b